  "cli_result_text": "Submitted the file set required for the dependency-graph impact analysis.\n\n**Key findings surfaced during retrieval, for context on the submission:**\n\n1. **log4j-to-slf4j bridge module** (`log4j-to-slf4j/`) binds the Log4j2 API to SLF4J via `SLF4JProvider` (registered through `META-INF/services/org.apache.logging.log4j.spi.Provider`), redirecting `LogManager`/`Logger` calls made against the Log4j2 API to whatever SLF4J backend (e.g., Logback) is present. `SLF4JLoggerContextFactory` and `MDCContextMap` are the actual bridge implementations, mutually exclusive with `log4j-slf4j-impl`/`log4j-slf4j2-impl` (the reverse-direction bridge, SLF4J\u2192Log4j2).\n\n2. **Spring Boot's detection logic** (`Log4J2LoggingSystem.Factory`) does *not* key off `log4j-api` or `log4j-to-slf4j` at all \u2014 it probes for `org.apache.logging.log4j.core.impl.Log4jContextFactory`, a `log4j-core` class. This means Spring Boot's auto-detection is insulated from the bridge-module restructuring as long as `log4j-core` stays on the classpath; the ordering in `spring.factories` (Logback \u2192 Log4j2 \u2192 JUL) is the actual tie-breaker when multiple systems are present.\n\n3. `log4j-spring-boot`'s own `Log4j2SpringBootLoggingSystem` (living in the log4j2 repo itself) directly extends Spring Boot's `Log4J2LoggingSystem`, forming the cross-repo coupling point.\n\nThe `IMPACT_REPORT.md` should be written from these files under `/workspace/analysis/`.",
